      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your concern and the need to appear adaptable and in control. You can develop specific protocols for handling disruptions and mistakes by following a proactive approach and demonstrating problem-solving skills.\n\n1. Document Responses to Input Changes: Create a template for quick responses when things change last-minute. It should include a brief acknowledgment, a commitment to a new deadline if necessary, and a detour to outline your ability to adjust and deliver under pressure.\n\n2. Have Standardized Reaction Phrases: Develop a set of pre-approved phrases to use when addressing unexpected issues. For example, \"I understand the urgency, my immediate thought is to...\" or \"Although I've encountered [problem], I am quickly devising a strategy to...\"\n\n3. Build a 'Disaster Plan': Pre-plan for likely unexpected events for various scenarios relevant to your role. By doing so, you'll have tangible solutions ready to present, showcasing foresight and preparedness.\n\n4. Reflect and Adapt: After each disruptive event, take a moment to jot down what happened, how it was handled, and what the outcome was. Use these reflections to continuously improve your reaction plans and to illustrate to your assessors that you learn and grow from these experiences.\n\n5. Rapid Problem-Solving Workshops: Periodically conduct",
      "source": "target",
